# verdict: satisfiable
# Source: ty mdtest bidirectional.md:1961
#   Bidirectional type inference / Overload evaluation (the `f9` function)
# ty: `sink(func, future)` is an error today. A TODO says that it should not
# be an error "once we conjoin constraints from all call arguments". These
# constraints have no error.
#
#   def sink(func: Callable[[], Union[Awaitable[T2], T2]],
#            future: Future[T2]) -> None
#   def f9(func: Callable[[], Union[Awaitable[T2], T2]]) -> Future[T2]:
#       future: Future[T2] = Future()
#       sink(func, future)
#
# Inside `f9`, its T2 is a fixed unknown type. A fresh class models it
# (CallerT2). The call gives the T2 of `sink` a fresh metavariable ?T. Each
# argument check is one assertion. The callback return is a union on both
# sides. CallerT2 must go to the bare ?T arm. Awaitable[CallerT2] can go to
# either arm: the Awaitable[?T] arm needs CallerT2 <: ?T, and the bare arm
# needs Awaitable[CallerT2] <: ?T. Future is invariant, so the second
# argument makes ?T equal CallerT2. Then only the Awaitable[?T] arm works.
# A solver that solves ?T from the first argument alone can put
# Awaitable[CallerT2] into ?T, and then the second argument fails.
#
# Variance: Future declares an invariant type parameter.
# Approximations: Awaitable (a protocol) is a nominal covariant class. The
# caller's T2 has no bound, so CallerT2 is not a subtype of Awaitable.
class Awaitable[+T];
class Future[T];
class CallerT2;
metavar ?T;

assert func: (() -> Awaitable[CallerT2] | CallerT2) <: (() -> Awaitable[?T] | ?T);
assert future: Future[CallerT2] <: Future[?T];
